 ## Operating characteristics
 *   **Memory:** The amount of memory required for a 3D buffer is obviously much greater than a 2D shadow depth buffer [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Advantages:** Avoids shadow aliasing problems encountered by Williams' 2D shadow depth buffer. Correctly handles semi-transparent shadows and penumbras. Correctly renders shadows cast by volumetrically defined objects on themselves, shadows cast by polygons on volumetric objects, and vice versa [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** High memory consumption due to the 3D nature of the buffer. The solution to self-shadowing aliasing (translating the buffer) reduces accuracy within small objects [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Advantages:** Avoids shadow aliasing problems encountered by Williams' 2D shadow depth buffer [S1, unofficial]. Correctly handles semi-transparent shadows and penumbras [S1, unofficial]. Correctly renders shadows cast by volumetrically defined objects on themselves, shadows cast by polygons on volumetric objects, and vice versa [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** High memory consumption due to the 3D nature of the buffer [S1, unofficial]. The solution to self-shadowing aliasing (translating the buffer) reduces accuracy within small objects [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method employs a two-pass approach similar to Williams [1978], but stores shadow information in a 3D light strength buffer instead of a 2D shadow depth buffer [S1, unofficial]. It is described in the context of rendering mixtures of geometric and volumetric data, where it also facilitates texture mapping techniques such as projecting textures through space onto volumetric surfaces [S1, unofficial].
